1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Here's a problem that can bite you the first time you use batching.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:08,000
You submit requests in a careful order, get results back, and they don't line up.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Batches don't return results in the order that you sent them.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,000
This lesson covers how a batch request is built, how custom ID fixes the problem,

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,000
and which parameters work inside a batch. Let's dive in.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Let's build a batch request from the pieces. A batch is a list of entries,

7
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:32,000
and each entry pairs a custom ID with params. The custom ID is a unique label you attach to

8
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:37,000
that request. Hold on to that idea, because it's the key to everything later.

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,000
The params is just a standard messages request body.

10
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:46,000
The same model, max tokens, messages, and tools you'd send on a normal call.

11
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,000
And there's a rule tying it together. Every custom ID in the batch has to be unique.

12
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:56,000
If you reuse one, your results get ambiguous, and the whole matching step falls apart.

13
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,000
Custom ID, params, and uniqueness. That's a batch entry.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Let's nail down the custom ID format, because the API enforces it strictly.

15
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,000
It has to be between one and 64 characters long.

16
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:13,000
The allowed characters are letters, numbers, hyphens, and underscores.

17
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Nothing else. No spaces. No slashes. No special symbols.

18
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:20,000
And as we said, it has to be unique across the batch.

19
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Here's a practical tip. Make your custom ID something meaningful.

20
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Like the primary key of the database row that request came from, or a document ID.

21
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:36,000
That way, when the results come back, matching each one to its source is trivial.

22
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,000
A random or reused custom ID just makes your life harder when you reassemble the output.

23
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:46,000
This brings us to the rule that matters most in the whole lesson.

24
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:51,000
When results come back, you match them by custom ID, never by position.

25
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:56,000
The right pattern is straightforward. Every result carries the custom ID you assigned.

26
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:01,000
So you use that key to look up which input it belongs to, no matter what order it arrived in.

27
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:06,000
The trap is assuming the third result matches the third input. It doesn't.

28
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Batch results aren't returned in submission order.

29
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:14,000
So lining them up by index silently corrupts your data.

30
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,000
And silently is the dangerous part.

31
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Nothing errors. You just get answers stapled to the wrong inputs.

32
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:22,000
Always key on custom ID.

33
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:26,000
One concrete failure mode makes this vivid.

34
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Say you batch a thousand support tickets for classification,

35
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:35,000
and you wrote a loop that zips your inputs and outputs together by position.

36
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:39,000
It will happily produce garbage, stapling each label to the wrong ticket,

37
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:42,000
and it won't throw a single error while doing it.

38
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Every row you write back now holds a plausible, but wrong, answer.

39
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:49,000
And nothing downstream can tell.

40
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,000
The fix is one line of discipline.

41
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,000
Load your inputs into a dictionary keyed by custom ID,

42
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:59,000
then walk the results and look each one up by the custom ID it carries.

43
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:03,000
Treat the arrival order as meaningless, because it is,

44
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,000
and build your mapping from that key every time.

45
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:09,000
Get that one habit right, and batches become reliable.

46
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Here's the misconception I most want to correct.

47
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:18,000
People assume a batch is a stripped-down mode that drops the good features. It isn't.

48
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:20,000
Batches keep almost everything.

49
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Vision works, so image inputs process exactly like they do synchronously.

50
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
Tool use works, and that includes every server tool,

51
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:33,000
web search, web fetch, and code execution.

52
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:34,000
Not just client tools.

53
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:40,000
System messages and full multi-turn conversations carry straight over.

54
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:43,000
And adaptive thinking runs inside batches too,

55
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:48,000
along with legacy extended thinking on the older models that still accept it.

56
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:50,000
So your reasoning heavy jobs are fine.

57
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:56,000
The mental model to drop is that batches lack tool use or vision. They don't.

58
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:01,000
A batch is a normal request that happens to run asynchronously and cheaply.

59
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:05,000
So what actually doesn't work in a batch?

60
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:06,000
The list is short and specific.

61
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Streaming is out.

62
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:13,000
Since a batch runs in the background, stream has no meaning and gets rejected.

63
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:16,000
The speed parameter, the fast mode flag, isn't allowed either.

64
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:22,000
Thread-related params, like store and the previous thread reference, are rejected.

65
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:25,000
And so are the cache hint and context hint parameters.

66
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,000
And max tokens has to be at least one, never zero.

67
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:31,000
Send any of those.

68
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,000
And the request fails validation.

69
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:39,000
This tends to bite when you build batch entries by reusing your synchronous request code,

70
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:42,000
because that code often sets stream to true.

71
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,000
And the leftover flag syncs the entry.

72
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:49,000
Strip those fields when you convert a call into a batch entry.

73
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:51,000
But notice what's not on this list.

74
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:55,000
Tool use, vision, thinking, and system messages are all fine.

75
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,000
One more practical detail on caching.

76
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:04,000
Prompt caching does work in a batch, but with a caveat rooted in how batches run.

77
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Requests in a batch execute concurrently and in any order.

78
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,000
So caching is best effort.

79
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:17,000
A short cache lifetime can easily expire before the requests that would have shared it actually run.

80
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:24,000
The practical move is to prefer the one-hour cache lifetime instead of the default short one

81
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:30,000
when your batch entries share a common prefix, like a big system prompt or a policy document.

82
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:35,000
That keeps the shared context warm long enough that later requests in the job can hit the cache.

83
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:38,000
It's a small setting with a real cost impact at scale.

84
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:41,000
Let's lock in the three things that trip people up,

85
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:44,000
because they're what you'll actually get wrong in practice.

86
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,000
First, unordered results.

87
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:51,000
They don't follow submission order, so you match everyone by custom ID,

88
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,000
never by position, or your data quietly misaligns.

89
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,000
Second, the limited parameters.

90
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:04,000
Stream, speed, the thread params, and the cache hint params all get rejected.

91
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,000
And max tokens has to be at least one.

92
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:10,000
Third, and this is the flip side, batches are still full-featured.

93
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:15,000
Tool use, vision, system messages, and thinking all work,

94
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,000
so don't treat a batch as some stripped-down mode.

95
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:22,000
Unordered results, limited params, still full-featured.

96
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:24,000
Those three keep you out of trouble.

97
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:26,000
Here's the bottom line.

98
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:33,000
A batch pairs a custom ID with a standard params body and runs the whole job asynchronously.

99
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:36,000
It matters because that one key, custom ID,

100
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:40,000
is how you safely match unordered results back to their inputs.

101
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:45,000
Just remember, batches still support tool use, vision, and thinking.

102
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:47,000
They only drop streaming and a few parameters.

103
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:51,000
That wraps up batch jobs and custom ID.

104
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:53,000
When you're ready, let's move on to the next video.

